  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Mid November warm and dry spell ends Sunday/Monday\n\nHigh clouds increase from south to north throughout the day\nwhich keeps max temps a few degrees cooler vs. Friday, but \nstill +10-20 degrees above daily climo. Rain chances increase \non Sunday as a complex low pressure system moves up the East \nCoast. There remains significant uncertainty in recent model \nguidance regarding how far west the rainfall can expand on the\nnorthwest side of the low track. Highest odds for rain remain\nfocused across the eastern portion of CPA Sunday into Sunday\nnight. A cold front will move through the Commonwealth overnight\nSunday into Monday and may be accompanied by a few showers.\nColder air filtering in behind the front should also generate\nsome lake effect/upslope response over the Allegheny Plateau\nwith temps cold enough for snow showers through Monday night. \n\nCumulative effects of stagnant air beneath departing high \npressure will result in a code orange air quality alert today \nfor the Lower Susquehanna Valley. \n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Colder with some winter weather potential \nTuesday through the Thanksgiving holiday weekend\n\nWe have high confidence in a noticeable cool down by Tuesday\nwith fcst highs 15 to 25 degrees lower than this weekend. Next\nweek will certainly feel more like late November with colder, \nmore seasonal temperatures Tuesday through the Thanksgiving \nholiday weekend. \n\nThere appears to be a general consensus toward increasing precip\npotential from mid to late week including on Thanksgiving Day.\nWhile timing and details are still not clear, there is some \nwinter weather potential with snow possible particularly in the\nhigher elevations.",
